<ReportItem port="443" svc_name="www" protocol="tcp" severity="4" pluginID="183891" pluginName="Cisco IOS XE Software Web UI Privilege Escalation (cisco-sa-iosxe-webui-privesc-j22SaA4z)" pluginFamily="CISCO">
<agent>unix</agent>
<cisa-known-exploited>2023/10/20</cisa-known-exploited>
<cve>CVE-2023-20198</cve>
<cve>CVE-2023-20273</cve>
<cvss3_base_score>10.0</cvss3_base_score>
<description>The Cisco IOS XE device has the HTTP/HTTPS server feature enabled and is running a vulnerable release. An unauthenticated attacker can create a privilege level 15 account and then use a second flaw to run commands as root. Implants were widely deployed through this pair of flaws in October 2023.</description>
<exploit_available>true</exploit_available>
<fname>183891.nasl</fname>
<plugin_name>Cisco IOS XE Software Web UI Privilege Escalation (cisco-sa-iosxe-webui-privesc-j22SaA4z)</plugin_name>
<plugin_output>Installed version : 17.6.4
Fixed version     : 17.6.6a
ip http secure-server : enabled</plugin_output>
<plugin_type>remote</plugin_type>
<risk_factor>Critical</risk_factor>
<see_also>https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2023-20198</see_also>
<see_also>https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2023-20273</see_also>
<solution>Upgrade to IOS XE 17.6.6a or later. Until then, disable the web UI with 'no ip http server' and 'no ip http secure-server'. Check the running configuration for unknown local user accounts.</solution>
<synopsis>The Cisco IOS XE device has the HTTP/HTTPS server feature enabled and is running a vulnerable release.</synopsis>
</ReportItem>
<ReportItem port="161" svc_name="snmp" protocol="udp" severity="3" pluginID="41028" pluginName="SNMP Agent Default Community Name (public)" pluginFamily="SNMP">
<agent>unix</agent>
<cve>CVE-1999-0517</cve>
<cvss3_base_score>7.5</cvss3_base_score>
<description>It is possible to obtain the default community name of the remote SNMP server. An attacker can read the device configuration, routing tables, and interface details, and on core-sw01 the community is read-write.</description>
<exploit_available>true</exploit_available>
<fname>41028.nasl</fname>
<plugin_name>SNMP Agent Default Community Name (public)</plugin_name>
<plugin_output>The remote SNMP server replies to the following default community strings:
  public  (core-sw01: RW)
  private (core-sw01: RW)</plugin_output>
<plugin_type>remote</plugin_type>
<risk_factor>High</risk_factor>
<see_also>https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-1999-0517</see_also>
<solution>Disable SNMP if it is not used. Otherwise, move to SNMPv3 with authPriv, or at minimum change the community string and limit access with an ACL to the monitoring server.</solution>
<synopsis>It is possible to obtain the default community name of the remote SNMP server.</synopsis>
</ReportItem>
<ReportItem port="443" svc_name="www" protocol="tcp" severity="2" pluginID="51192" pluginName="SSL Certificate Cannot Be Trusted" pluginFamily="General">
<agent>unix</agent>
<cvss3_base_score>6.5</cvss3_base_score>
<description>The X.509 certificate chain for this service is not signed by a recognized certificate authority. Users who learn to click through certificate warnings cannot tell a real service from a man-in-the-middle.</description>
<fname>51192.nasl</fname>
<plugin_name>SSL Certificate Cannot Be Trusted</plugin_name>
<plugin_output>Subject : CN=RB-PRINT01
Issuer  : CN=RB-PRINT01 (self-signed)
Not After : 2024-08-14 (expired)</plugin_output>
<plugin_type>remote</plugin_type>
<risk_factor>Medium</risk_factor>
<solution>Issue certificates from the district's internal CA (or a public CA for external services) and deploy the CA root to managed devices.</solution>
<synopsis>The X.509 certificate chain for this service is not signed by a recognized certificate authority.</synopsis>
</ReportItem>
<ReportItem port="23" svc_name="telnet" protocol="tcp" severity="2" pluginID="42263" pluginName="Unencrypted Telnet Server" pluginFamily="Misc.">
<agent>unix</agent>
<cvss3_base_score>6.5</cvss3_base_score>
<description>The remote host is running a Telnet server over an unencrypted channel. Logins, passwords, and commands are sent in cleartext and can be captured by anyone on the path.</description>
<fname>42263.nasl</fname>
<plugin_name>Unencrypted Telnet Server</plugin_name>
<plugin_output>Banner: User Access Verification\r\nUsername:</plugin_output>
<plugin_type>remote</plugin_type>
<risk_factor>Medium</risk_factor>
<solution>Disable Telnet ('transport input ssh' on all VTY lines) and use SSH version 2.</solution>
<synopsis>The remote host is running a Telnet server over an unencrypted channel.</synopsis>
</ReportItem>
<ReportItem port="22" svc_name="ssh" protocol="tcp" severity="2" pluginID="187315" pluginName="SSH Terrapin Prefix Truncation Weakness" pluginFamily="Misc.">
<agent>unix</agent>
<cve>CVE-2023-48795</cve>
<cvss3_base_score>5.9</cvss3_base_score>
<description>The remote SSH server supports ChaCha20-Poly1305 or CBC ciphers with Encrypt-then-MAC and does not support strict key exchange. A man-in-the-middle can drop messages at the start of the session and downgrade security features.</description>
<fname>187315.nasl</fname>
<plugin_name>SSH Terrapin Prefix Truncation Weakness</plugin_name>
<plugin_output>Supports following ChaCha20-Poly1305 Client to Server algorithm : chacha20-poly1305@openssh.com
Strict KEX : not supported</plugin_output>
<plugin_type>remote</plugin_type>
<risk_factor>Medium</risk_factor>
<see_also>https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2023-48795</see_also>
<solution>Update the SSH server to a release that supports strict KEX (OpenSSH 9.6 or vendor backport), or disable chacha20-poly1305@openssh.com and *-etm@openssh.com MACs.</solution>
<synopsis>The remote SSH server supports ChaCha20-Poly1305 or CBC ciphers with Encrypt-then-MAC and does not support strict key exchange.</synopsis>
</ReportItem>
<ReportItem port="22" svc_name="ssh" protocol="tcp" severity="1" pluginID="153953" pluginName="SSH Weak Key Exchange Algorithms Enabled" pluginFamily="Misc.">
<agent>unix</agent>
<cvss3_base_score>3.7</cvss3_base_score>
<description>The remote SSH server allows key exchange algorithms that RFC 9142 marks as SHOULD NOT or MUST NOT, such as diffie-hellman-group1-sha1 and diffie-hellman-group-exchange-sha1.</description>
<fname>153953.nasl</fname>
<plugin_name>SSH Weak Key Exchange Algorithms Enabled</plugin_name>
<plugin_output>diffie-hellman-group1-sha1
diffie-hellman-group14-sha1
diffie-hellman-group-exchange-sha1</plugin_output>
<plugin_type>remote</plugin_type>
<risk_factor>Low</risk_factor>
<solution>Remove the weak algorithms from the server's KexAlgorithms list ('ip ssh dh min size 2048' and 'ip ssh server algorithm kex' on IOS XE).</solution>
<synopsis>The remote SSH server allows key exchange algorithms that RFC 9142 marks as SHOULD NOT or MUST NOT, such as diffie-hellman-group1-sha1 and diffie-hellman-group-exchange-sha1.</synopsis>
</ReportItem>
<ReportItem port="22" svc_name="ssh" protocol="tcp" severity="1" pluginID="71049" pluginName="SSH Weak MAC Algorithms Enabled" pluginFamily="Misc.">
<agent>unix</agent>
<cvss3_base_score>2.6</cvss3_base_score>
<description>The remote SSH server is configured to allow MD5 or 96-bit MAC algorithms.</description>
<fname>71049.nasl</fname>
<plugin_name>SSH Weak MAC Algorithms Enabled</plugin_name>
<plugin_output>hmac-md5
hmac-md5-96
hmac-sha1-96</plugin_output>
<plugin_type>remote</plugin_type>
<risk_factor>Low</risk_factor>
<solution>Disable MD5 and 96-bit MAC algorithms; allow hmac-sha2-256 and hmac-sha2-512.</solution>
<synopsis>The remote SSH server is configured to allow MD5 or 96-bit MAC algorithms.</synopsis>
</ReportItem>
<ReportItem port="22" svc_name="ssh" protocol="tcp" severity="1" pluginID="70658" pluginName="SSH Server CBC Mode Ciphers Enabled" pluginFamily="Misc.">
<agent>unix</agent>
<cvss3_base_score>2.6</cvss3_base_score>
<description>The SSH server allows Cipher Block Chaining (CBC) encryption, which may let an attacker recover plaintext from the ciphertext under specific conditions.</description>
<fname>70658.nasl</fname>
<plugin_name>SSH Server CBC Mode Ciphers Enabled</plugin_name>
<plugin_output>aes128-cbc
aes256-cbc
3des-cbc</plugin_output>
<plugin_type>remote</plugin_type>
<risk_factor>Low</risk_factor>
<solution>Disable CBC mode ciphers and allow CTR or GCM ciphers.</solution>
<synopsis>The SSH server allows Cipher Block Chaining (CBC) encryption, which may let an attacker recover plaintext from the ciphertext under specific conditions.</synopsis>
</ReportItem>
<ReportItem port="0" svc_name="general" protocol="icmp" severity="1" pluginID="10114" pluginName="ICMP Timestamp Request Remote Date Disclosure" pluginFamily="General">
<agent>unix</agent>
<cve>CVE-1999-0524</cve>
<cvss3_base_score>2.1</cvss3_base_score>
<description>The remote host answers ICMP timestamp requests, which lets an attacker learn the date set on the target. This can help defeat time-based authentication protocols.</description>
<fname>10114.nasl</fname>
<plugin_name>ICMP Timestamp Request Remote Date Disclosure</plugin_name>
<plugin_output>The difference between the local and remote clocks is 2 seconds.</plugin_output>
<plugin_type>remote</plugin_type>
<risk_factor>Low</risk_factor>
<see_also>https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-1999-0524</see_also>
<solution>Filter ICMP timestamp requests (13) and replies (14) at the host or network firewall.</solution>
<synopsis>The remote host answers ICMP timestamp requests, which lets an attacker learn the date set on the target.</synopsis>
</ReportItem>
</ReportHost>
