<ReportItem port="636" svc_name="ldap" protocol="tcp" severity="3" pluginID="42873" pluginName="SSL Medium Strength Cipher Suites Supported (SWEET32)" pluginFamily="General">
<agent>windows</agent>
<cve>CVE-2016-2183</cve>
<cvss3_base_score>7.5</cvss3_base_score>
<description>The remote host supports SSL ciphers that use 64-bit block ciphers (3DES). An attacker on the same network who can capture a long-lived session can recover plaintext with a birthday attack.</description>
<exploit_available>true</exploit_available>
<fname>42873.nasl</fname>
<plugin_name>SSL Medium Strength Cipher Suites Supported (SWEET32)</plugin_name>
<plugin_output>Medium Strength Ciphers (&gt; 64-bit and &lt; 112-bit key, or 3DES)
  ECDHE-RSA-DES-CBC3-SHA   Kx=ECDH Au=RSA Enc=3DES-CBC(168) Mac=SHA1
  DES-CBC3-SHA             Kx=RSA  Au=RSA Enc=3DES-CBC(168) Mac=SHA1</plugin_output>
<plugin_type>remote</plugin_type>
<risk_factor>High</risk_factor>
<see_also>https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2016-2183</see_also>
<solution>Disable 3DES and other 64-bit block ciphers in the SChannel configuration (or application TLS settings) and allow only AES-GCM suites with TLS 1.2 or later.</solution>
<synopsis>The remote host supports SSL ciphers that use 64-bit block ciphers (3DES).</synopsis>
</ReportItem>
<ReportItem port="636" svc_name="ldap" protocol="tcp" severity="2" pluginID="104743" pluginName="TLS Version 1.0 Protocol Detection" pluginFamily="Service detection">
<agent>windows</agent>
<cvss3_base_score>6.5</cvss3_base_score>
<description>The remote service accepts connections encrypted using TLS 1.0, which has known design flaws. PCI DSS and most current guidance require disabling it.</description>
<fname>104743.nasl</fname>
<plugin_name>TLS Version 1.0 Protocol Detection</plugin_name>
<plugin_output>TLSv1.0 is enabled and the server supports at least one cipher.</plugin_output>
<plugin_type>remote</plugin_type>
<risk_factor>Medium</risk_factor>
<solution>Disable TLS 1.0 and 1.1 and enable TLS 1.2 and 1.3. On Windows, set the SChannel protocol registry keys or use IIS Crypto; confirm SIS and finance integrations support TLS 1.2 first.</solution>
<synopsis>The remote service accepts connections encrypted using TLS 1.0, which has known design flaws.</synopsis>
</ReportItem>
