<ReportItem port="443" svc_name="www" protocol="tcp" severity="3" pluginID="165703" pluginName="Microsoft Exchange Server Multiple Vulnerabilities (ProxyNotShell)" pluginFamily="Windows">
<agent>windows</agent>
<cisa-known-exploited>2022/10/21</cisa-known-exploited>
<cve>CVE-2022-41040</cve>
<cve>CVE-2022-41082</cve>
<cvss3_base_score>8.8</cvss3_base_score>
<description>The Exchange Server installation is affected by a server-side request forgery flaw that an authenticated attacker can chain with a PowerShell remote code execution flaw. Any mailbox credential is enough to start the chain.</description>
<exploit_available>true</exploit_available>
<fname>165703.nasl</fname>
<plugin_name>Microsoft Exchange Server Multiple Vulnerabilities (ProxyNotShell)</plugin_name>
<plugin_output>Product           : Exchange Server 2016 CU23
Installed build   : 15.1.2507.12
Fixed build       : 15.1.2507.16</plugin_output>
<plugin_type>local</plugin_type>
<risk_factor>High</risk_factor>
<see_also>https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2022-41040</see_also>
<see_also>https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2022-41082</see_also>
<solution>Apply the November 2022 Exchange Server Security Update (or later) for Exchange 2016 CU23. Exchange 2016 reached end of support in October 2025, so plan to move remaining mailboxes to Exchange Online or Exchange Server Subscription Edition.</solution>
<synopsis>The Exchange Server installation is affected by a server-side request forgery flaw that an authenticated attacker can chain with a PowerShell remote code execution flaw.</synopsis>
</ReportItem>
<ReportItem port="443" svc_name="www" protocol="tcp" severity="2" pluginID="104743" pluginName="TLS Version 1.0 Protocol Detection" pluginFamily="Service detection">
<agent>windows</agent>
<cvss3_base_score>6.5</cvss3_base_score>
<description>The remote service accepts connections encrypted using TLS 1.0, which has known design flaws. PCI DSS and most current guidance require disabling it.</description>
<fname>104743.nasl</fname>
<plugin_name>TLS Version 1.0 Protocol Detection</plugin_name>
<plugin_output>TLSv1.0 is enabled and the server supports at least one cipher.</plugin_output>
<plugin_type>remote</plugin_type>
<risk_factor>Medium</risk_factor>
<solution>Disable TLS 1.0 and 1.1 and enable TLS 1.2 and 1.3. On Windows, set the SChannel protocol registry keys or use IIS Crypto; confirm SIS and finance integrations support TLS 1.2 first.</solution>
<synopsis>The remote service accepts connections encrypted using TLS 1.0, which has known design flaws.</synopsis>
</ReportItem>
</ReportHost>
