<ReportItem port="9191" svc_name="www" protocol="tcp" severity="4" pluginID="174150" pluginName="PaperCut NG/MF Multiple Vulnerabilities (ZDI-CAN-18987)" pluginFamily="CGI abuses">
<agent>windows</agent>
<cisa-known-exploited>2023/05/12</cisa-known-exploited>
<cve>CVE-2023-27350</cve>
<cvss3_base_score>9.8</cvss3_base_score>
<description>The version of PaperCut MF installed on the remote host is affected by an improper access control flaw in the SetupCompleted class. An unauthenticated attacker can bypass authentication and execute arbitrary code as SYSTEM. CISA and FBI reported this flaw being exploited against the education sector.</description>
<exploit_available>true</exploit_available>
<fname>174150.nasl</fname>
<plugin_name>PaperCut NG/MF Multiple Vulnerabilities (ZDI-CAN-18987)</plugin_name>
<plugin_output>Path              : C:\Program Files\PaperCut MF\
Installed version : 21.2.4
Fixed version     : 21.2.11</plugin_output>
<plugin_type>remote</plugin_type>
<risk_factor>Critical</risk_factor>
<see_also>https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2023-27350</see_also>
<solution>Upgrade PaperCut MF/NG to 20.1.7, 21.2.11, 22.0.9, or later. Block external access to tcp/9191 and tcp/9192 and restrict the admin console to IT management addresses.</solution>
<synopsis>The version of PaperCut MF installed on the remote host is affected by an improper access control flaw in the SetupCompleted class.</synopsis>
</ReportItem>
<ReportItem port="445" svc_name="cifs" protocol="tcp" severity="3" pluginID="151440" pluginName="Windows Print Spooler Remote Code Execution (PrintNightmare)" pluginFamily="Windows">
<agent>windows</agent>
<cisa-known-exploited>2022/05/03</cisa-known-exploited>
<cve>CVE-2021-34527</cve>
<cvss3_base_score>8.8</cvss3_base_score>
<description>The Print Spooler service on the remote host is affected by a remote code execution flaw. An authenticated user can install a malicious printer driver and run code as SYSTEM. On domain controllers this leads to domain compromise.</description>
<exploit_available>true</exploit_available>
<fname>151440.nasl</fname>
<plugin_name>Windows Print Spooler Remote Code Execution (PrintNightmare)</plugin_name>
<plugin_output>Print Spooler service : Running
RestrictDriverInstallationToAdministrators : not set
Point and Print NoWarningNoElevationOnInstall : 1</plugin_output>
<plugin_type>local</plugin_type>
<risk_factor>High</risk_factor>
<see_also>https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2021-34527</see_also>
<solution>Apply the July 2021 (or later) cumulative update and set RestrictDriverInstallationToAdministrators = 1. Disable the Print Spooler service on domain controllers and any server that does not print.</solution>
<synopsis>The Print Spooler service on the remote host is affected by a remote code execution flaw.</synopsis>
</ReportItem>
<ReportItem port="9192" svc_name="www" protocol="tcp" severity="3" pluginID="42873" pluginName="SSL Medium Strength Cipher Suites Supported (SWEET32)" pluginFamily="General">
<agent>windows</agent>
<cve>CVE-2016-2183</cve>
<cvss3_base_score>7.5</cvss3_base_score>
<description>The remote host supports SSL ciphers that use 64-bit block ciphers (3DES). An attacker on the same network who can capture a long-lived session can recover plaintext with a birthday attack.</description>
<exploit_available>true</exploit_available>
<fname>42873.nasl</fname>
<plugin_name>SSL Medium Strength Cipher Suites Supported (SWEET32)</plugin_name>
<plugin_output>Medium Strength Ciphers (&gt; 64-bit and &lt; 112-bit key, or 3DES)
  ECDHE-RSA-DES-CBC3-SHA   Kx=ECDH Au=RSA Enc=3DES-CBC(168) Mac=SHA1
  DES-CBC3-SHA             Kx=RSA  Au=RSA Enc=3DES-CBC(168) Mac=SHA1</plugin_output>
<plugin_type>remote</plugin_type>
<risk_factor>High</risk_factor>
<see_also>https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2016-2183</see_also>
<solution>Disable 3DES and other 64-bit block ciphers in the SChannel configuration (or application TLS settings) and allow only AES-GCM suites with TLS 1.2 or later.</solution>
<synopsis>The remote host supports SSL ciphers that use 64-bit block ciphers (3DES).</synopsis>
</ReportItem>
<ReportItem port="161" svc_name="snmp" protocol="udp" severity="3" pluginID="41028" pluginName="SNMP Agent Default Community Name (public)" pluginFamily="SNMP">
<agent>windows</agent>
<cve>CVE-1999-0517</cve>
<cvss3_base_score>7.5</cvss3_base_score>
<description>It is possible to obtain the default community name of the remote SNMP server. An attacker can read the device configuration, routing tables, and interface details, and on core-sw01 the community is read-write.</description>
<exploit_available>true</exploit_available>
<fname>41028.nasl</fname>
<plugin_name>SNMP Agent Default Community Name (public)</plugin_name>
<plugin_output>The remote SNMP server replies to the following default community strings:
  public  (core-sw01: RW)
  private (core-sw01: RW)</plugin_output>
<plugin_type>remote</plugin_type>
<risk_factor>High</risk_factor>
<see_also>https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-1999-0517</see_also>
<solution>Disable SNMP if it is not used. Otherwise, move to SNMPv3 with authPriv, or at minimum change the community string and limit access with an ACL to the monitoring server.</solution>
<synopsis>It is possible to obtain the default community name of the remote SNMP server.</synopsis>
</ReportItem>
<ReportItem port="445" svc_name="cifs" protocol="tcp" severity="2" pluginID="57608" pluginName="SMB Signing not required" pluginFamily="Misc.">
<agent>windows</agent>
<cvss3_base_score>5.3</cvss3_base_score>
<description>Signing is not required on the remote SMB server. An unauthenticated attacker on the network can carry out man-in-the-middle and NTLM relay attacks against the SMB server.</description>
<fname>57608.nasl</fname>
<plugin_name>SMB Signing not required</plugin_name>
<plugin_output>Host does not require SMB signing (SMBv2 negotiate: signing enabled, not required).</plugin_output>
<plugin_type>remote</plugin_type>
<risk_factor>Medium</risk_factor>
<solution>Enforce message signing with Group Policy: 'Microsoft network server: Digitally sign communications (always)' and the matching client setting. Test against copiers and older NAS devices first.</solution>
<synopsis>Signing is not required on the remote SMB server.</synopsis>
</ReportItem>
<ReportItem port="9192" svc_name="www" protocol="tcp" severity="2" pluginID="51192" pluginName="SSL Certificate Cannot Be Trusted" pluginFamily="General">
<agent>windows</agent>
<cvss3_base_score>6.5</cvss3_base_score>
<description>The X.509 certificate chain for this service is not signed by a recognized certificate authority. Users who learn to click through certificate warnings cannot tell a real service from a man-in-the-middle.</description>
<fname>51192.nasl</fname>
<plugin_name>SSL Certificate Cannot Be Trusted</plugin_name>
<plugin_output>Subject : CN=RB-PRINT01
Issuer  : CN=RB-PRINT01 (self-signed)
Not After : 2024-08-14 (expired)</plugin_output>
<plugin_type>remote</plugin_type>
<risk_factor>Medium</risk_factor>
<solution>Issue certificates from the district's internal CA (or a public CA for external services) and deploy the CA root to managed devices.</solution>
<synopsis>The X.509 certificate chain for this service is not signed by a recognized certificate authority.</synopsis>
</ReportItem>
<ReportItem port="9191" svc_name="www" protocol="tcp" severity="2" pluginID="11213" pluginName="HTTP TRACE / TRACK Methods Allowed" pluginFamily="Web Servers">
<agent>windows</agent>
<cvss3_base_score>5.3</cvss3_base_score>
<description>The remote web server supports the TRACE and/or TRACK methods, which echo the request back to the client and can expose headers such as cookies in cross-site tracing attacks.</description>
<fname>11213.nasl</fname>
<plugin_name>HTTP TRACE / TRACK Methods Allowed</plugin_name>
<plugin_output>TRACE /Nessus123456.html HTTP/1.1 -&gt; HTTP/1.1 200 OK (request echoed)</plugin_output>
<plugin_type>remote</plugin_type>
<risk_factor>Medium</risk_factor>
<solution>Disable TRACE and TRACK in the web server configuration (TraceEnable off for Apache).</solution>
<synopsis>The remote web server supports the TRACE and/or TRACK methods, which echo the request back to the client and can expose headers such as cookies in cross-site tracing attacks.</synopsis>
</ReportItem>
</ReportHost>
